he reason would be indifferent. But this seems convoluted. Alternatively, perhaps 'the outcome with the minimum absolute value' is taken across both lotteries: 0 in A is smallest, so sure outcome = 0. Then for each lottery, compute differences from 0. In A: 0 - 0 = 0. In B: 6000 - 0 = 6000 and -6000 - 0 = -6000, weighted by 0.5: expected difference = 0.5*6000 + 0.5*(-6000) = 0. Then sum: 0 + 0 = 0 for both. Again, indifferent. But the reason says 'the outcome with the minimum absolute value is considered a sure outcome' — likely within the lottery. So for A: sure outcome = 0. No other outcomes, so no differences. Value = 0. For B: both outcomes have |6000|, so minimum absolute value is 6000. So sure outcome = 6000? But that's arbitrary — why not -6000? The rule doesn't specify which one if tied. This makes the reason ambiguous. But assuming it picks one, say 6000. Then differences: 6000 - 6000 = 0 (prob 0.5), -6000 - 6000 = -12000 (prob 0.5). Expected difference = -6000. Sum = 6000 + (-6000) = 0. Same as A. Still indifferent. If sure outcome is -6000, differences: 6000 - (-6000) = 12000, -6000 - (-6000) = 0. Expected difference = 6000. Sum = -6000 + 6000 = 0. Same. So regardless, both lotteries evaluate to 0. The reason is indifferent. But the verbal report indicates a choice was made. So the reason may not be decisive here, but it can still be applied — it just yields indifference.",
